<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-resonance" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Resonance</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       INTRO PROSE: RECALL AND NAME RESONANCE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    In <xref ref="sec-external-force-examples"/> we found two general solutions
    to <m>x'' + kx = 50\cos(3t)</m>:
    <md>
      <mrow>k = 8: \quad x(t) \amp= \underbrace{c_1\cos(\sqrt{8}\,t) + c_2\sin(\sqrt{8}\,t)}_{x_c}
        + \underbrace{-50\cos(3t)}_{x_p}</mrow>
      <mrow>k = 9: \quad x(t) \amp= \underbrace{c_1\cos(3t) + c_2\sin(3t)}_{x_c}
        + \underbrace{\tfrac{50}{6}\,t\sin(3t)}_{x_p}</mrow>
    </md>
    As you noticed, the <m>k = 9</m> case landed in the case of duplication because
    the natural frequency <m>\omega_0 = \sqrt{9} = 3</m> equaled the forcing frequency
    <m>\omega = 3</m>. When the natural and forcing frequencies align like this,
    we say the system is in <alert>resonance</alert>.
    We will see shortly what this means physically.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       THEOREM: GENERAL SOLUTION IN BOTH CASES
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Putting together what we know from undetermined coefficients,
    the general solution to the forced undamped system looks quite
    different depending on whether resonance occurs.
  </p>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-resonance-solution">
    <title>General Solution: Resonance vs. Non-Resonance</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the forced undamped system
        <me>mx'' + kx = F_0\cos(\omega t) + G_0\sin(\omega t), \quad \omega_0 = \sqrt{\frac{k}{m}}.</me>
      </p>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>
            <alert>Non-resonance</alert> (<m>\omega \neq \omega_0</m>):
            <me>x(t) = \underbrace{c_1\cos(\omega_0 t) + c_2\sin(\omega_0 t)}_{x_c}
              + \underbrace{A\cos(\omega t) + B\sin(\omega t)}_{x_p}.</me>
          </p></li>
          <li><p>
            <alert>Resonance</alert> (<m>\omega = \omega_0</m>):
            <me>x(t) = \underbrace{c_1\cos(\omega_0 t) + c_2\sin(\omega_0 t)}_{x_c}
              + \underbrace{t\bigl(A\cos(\omega_0 t) + B\sin(\omega_0 t)\bigr)}_{x_p}.</me>
          </p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       NON-RESONANCE CASE: PROSE + STATIC GRAPH
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    In the non-resonance case, both <m>x_c</m> and <m>x_p</m> are pure sinusoidal functions,
    just at different frequencies (<m>\omega_0</m> and <m>\omega</m> respectively).
    Their sum oscillates with bounded amplitude forever. Here is the <m>k = 8</m>
    solution from the previous page:
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-nonresonance-graph">
    <caption>
      The non-resonance solution <m>x(t) = \cos(\sqrt{8}\,t) - 50\cos(3t)</m>
      (with <m>c_1 = 1</m>, <m>c_2 = 0</m>). Two sinusoidal functions at different frequencies
      add together; the amplitude stays bounded.
    </caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-nonresonance-graph"
      iframe="ch3-graph/static-resonance-k8.html"
      width="100%" aspect="5:2">
      <description>
        A graph of x of t equals cosine of square root 8 times t minus 50 cosine of 3t,
        for t from 0 to 10. The curve oscillates with roughly constant amplitude,
        showing the superposition of two sinusoidal functions at different frequencies.
        The amplitude stays bounded throughout, neither growing nor decaying.
      </description>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       MC CHECKPOINT: WHAT HAPPENS AS t GETS LARGE?
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Now look at the resonance case. The particular solution is
    <m>x_p = \frac{50}{6}\,t\sin(3t)</m>. Before looking at the graph, take a guess.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mc-resonance-behavior">
    <statement>
      <p>
        As <m>t</m> gets larger and larger, what happens to the resonance solution?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>The oscillations get larger and larger; the amplitude grows without bound.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Yes! The factor of <m>t</m> in <m>x_p</m> acts like a growing envelope:
            the amplitude of the oscillations scales with <m>t</m>, so it grows
            without bound as <m>t \to \infty</m>.
            <m>\checkmark</m>
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>The oscillations stay roughly the same size.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            This would be true if <m>x_p</m> were a pure sinusoidal function. But the factor
            of <m>t</m> means the amplitude scales with time, so it cannot stay bounded.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>The oscillations gradually die out and <m>x(t) \to 0</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Decaying oscillations would require a negative exponential factor like
            <m>e^{-\alpha t}</m>. Here we have a factor of <m>t</m> growing in the positive
            direction, so the amplitude increases rather than decreases.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>The solution eventually becomes periodic and repeats.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            A periodic solution would require a bounded, repeating pattern.
            The factor of <m>t</m> prevents that: each cycle is bigger than the last,
            so the solution never repeats.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       POST-MC PROSE: EXPLAIN t FACTOR, THEN GRAPH
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    In the resonance case the particular solution is
    <me>x_p = \underbrace{t\bigl(A\cos(\omega_0 t) + B\sin(\omega_0 t)\bigr)}_{\text{factor of } t \text{ out front}}.</me>
    The factor of <m>t</m> multiplies the sinusoidal part, so as <m>t</m> grows
    the amplitude grows with it. The complementary solution <m>x_c</m> is just
    a bounded sinusoidal function riding on top, so the full solution
    <m>x = x_c + x_p</m> is dominated by the growing <m>x_p</m>.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ANIMATED RESONANCE GRAPH
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <figure xml:id="fig-resonance-animated">
    <caption>
      The full resonance solution <m>x(t) = \cos(3t) + \frac{50}{6}\,t\sin(3t)</m>
      (with <m>c_1 = 1</m>, <m>c_2 = 0</m>).
      The dashed red curves show the envelope <m>\pm\frac{50}{6}\,t</m> from <m>x_p</m>.
      Press Play to watch the amplitude grow.
    </caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-resonance-animated"
      iframe="ch3-graph/interactive-resonance-full.html"
      width="100%" aspect="5:3.2">
      <description>
        An animated graph of the full resonance solution x of t equals cosine of 3t
        plus 50 over 6 times t times sine of 3t, with c1 equals 1 and c2 equals 0.
        As the animation plays the curve traces out from left to right. Early on the
        bounded complementary solution is visible, but the growing particular solution
        quickly dominates. Dashed red envelope curves equal to plus and minus 50 over 6
        times t spread outward, and by t equals 10 the amplitude is much larger than
        at the start, illustrating that resonance causes unbounded growth.
      </description>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       EXAMPLE: FINDING THE RESONANCE FREQUENCY
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <example xml:id="example-resonance-frequency">
    <title>Finding the Resonance Frequency</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose <m>m = 1</m>, <m>k = 16</m>, and the external force is
        <m>F_0\cos(\omega t)</m>. For what forcing frequency <m>\omega</m>
        does resonance occur?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Resonance occurs when the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency
        of the system, <m>\omega = \omega_0 = \sqrt{k/m}</m>.
        What value of <m>\omega</m> makes this true for the given <m>m</m> and <m>k</m>?
      </p>
    </hint>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Resonance occurs when <m>\omega = \omega_0 = \sqrt{k/m} = \sqrt{16} = 4</m>.
        So we need <m>\omega = 4</m> (taking the positive value).
      </p>
      <p>
        Note that <m>\omega = 4</m> is the circular frequency. The ordinary frequency
        (in cycles per unit time) is <m>\frac{\omega}{2\pi} = \frac{4}{2\pi} = \frac{2}{\pi}</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       KEY TAKEAWAYS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <conclusion>
    <title>Key Takeaways</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Resonance</alert> occurs when the forcing frequency <m>\omega</m>
          equals the natural frequency <m>\omega_0 = \sqrt{k/m}</m>. Mathematically,
          this is exactly the case of duplication for undetermined coefficients.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Non-resonance</alert> (<m>\omega \neq \omega_0</m>): the solution is a
          bounded superposition of pure sinusoidal functions at different frequencies.
          The amplitude stays constant forever.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Resonance</alert> (<m>\omega = \omega_0</m>): the particular solution
          picks up a factor of <m>t</m>, which forces the amplitude to grow without bound
          as <m>t \to \infty</m>. In a physical system this means the oscillations grow
          larger and larger over time with no limit.
        </p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </conclusion>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
